---
import LearningGuideLayout from "../../layouts/LearningGuideLayout.astro";
import GuideFigure from "../../components/GuideFigure.astro";
---

<LearningGuideLayout
  title="How to Add Custom Interactions to FPE Objects in Godot"
  description="Add proximity, ray-cast, or direct-input interactions to Blender-authored FPE doors, switches, pickups, and props in Godot 4."
  summary="FPE can carry the repeated identity and setup from Blender; your game can add the unique interaction. Doors, buttons, notes, and pickups still benefit from one small contract: can the player interact, what prompt appears, and what happens next?"
  relatedSlugs={[
    "godot-trigger-system",
    "holdable-items-godot",
    "dialogue-triggers-godot",
  ]}
  godotSource={{
    label: "Ray casting",
    href: "https://docs.godotengine.org/en/stable/tutorials/physics/ray-casting.html",
  }}
>
  <div class="guide-summary">
    <strong>Use Blender commands before writing code.</strong> FPE triggers already
    support Enter, Exit, Interaction, Deposit, Withdraw, Hold, Release, Holdable
    Items Available/Unavailable, and Hold Zone Interaction. Scene Event commands
    can dispatch events, play or stop animations, control speakers and cameras, load
    levels or sub-scenes, start conversations, pause activities, delete groups, and
    restore player controls. Add custom Godot code only when your mechanic goes beyond
    that catalog.
  </div>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/trigger-events-panel.png"
    alt="FPE Trigger Events panel showing player interaction and a named event"
    caption="Start by checking the interaction FPE can already author. Add GDScript only when the object needs behavior beyond these built-in trigger choices."
  />
  <h2>Define a tiny interaction contract</h2><p>
    An interactable should answer whether it is currently usable, provide prompt
    text, and perform one action. Keep player input out of the door script; the
    player finds a target and asks it to interact.
  </p>
  <pre><code>class_name Interactable
extends Node3D

@export var prompt := "Use"

func can_interact(_actor: Node) -&gt; bool:
    return true

func interact(_actor: Node) -&gt; void:
    pass</code></pre>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/blender/trigger-invisible-checkboxes.png"
    alt="Invisible trigger volume placed around an interactive Blender object"
    caption="A proximity interaction begins as a deliberately shaped trigger volume around the usable part of the prop."
  />
  <h2>Three useful detection patterns</h2><h3>Proximity</h3><p>
    An <code>Area3D</code> tracks nearby candidates. This is forgiving and works
    well for cozy games, controller input, and objects that should announce themselves.
    Pick the closest or highest-priority candidate when several overlap.
  </p><h3>View-centered ray cast</h3><p>
    A <code>RayCast3D</code> from the camera gives precise first-person selection.
    Configure it to collide with the layers used by interactive physics bodies or
    areas, and walk from the collider to its interaction component.
  </p><h3>Direct pointer input</h3><p>
    Godot can deliver input events to a <code>CollisionObject3D</code> when object
    picking is enabled. This is a good fit for point-and-click scenes, but less convenient
    for keyboard or controller navigation.
  </p>
  <GuideFigure
    src="/assets/images/guides/godot/fpe-stage-editor.png"
    alt="Godot editor with the FoldedPaperEngine node in the stable Stage scene"
    caption="Attach custom components in the stable Godot scene or instantiate them around imported content; do not hand-edit generated imports."
  />
  <h2>Step-by-step implementation</h2><ol>
    <li>
      Create the interaction contract and implement it on one simple switch.
    </li><li>
      Add one detector to the player and keep its current target in a single
      property.
    </li><li>Show prompt text from that current target.</li><li>
      Map a project input action named <code>use</code> and call the target only
      on the pressed edge.
    </li><li>
      Make state changes idempotent: an already-open door should not start five
      overlapping animations.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul>
    <li>
      Putting bespoke ray-cast logic in every object instead of one player-side
      detector.
    </li><li>
      Assuming the ray collider is the scripted root; imported meshes often have
      collider children.
    </li><li>
      Leaving prompts visible after the target is freed or disabled.
    </li><li>Hard-coding key names instead of using an Input Map action.</li>
  </ul>
  <Fragment slot="fpe"
    ><p>
      Start in Blender's <strong>Trigger Events</strong> and <strong
        >Scene Events</strong
      > panels. If the available trigger types and commands can express the mechanic,
      you get a re-exportable artist-owned setup without custom code. For a genuinely
      unique mechanic, keep the imported FPE identity and add a Godot interaction
      component or a listener for the named event. FPE handles the repeated setup;
      your code owns what makes the object special.
    </p><p>
      <a href="/docs/fpe-triggers-commands.html"
        >See every FPE trigger type and Scene Event command</a
      >.
    </p></Fragment
  >
</LearningGuideLayout>
